# Netlify: the files in the published folder that are not part of the site answer the not-found
# page. `404!` rather than `404`: a plain rule is shadowed by a file at the same path, and these
# are files that can be there. Netlify never serves _headers or _redirects itself; they are listed
# so the three configurations refuse the same paths, and so are README.md, .git/ and deploy/, which
# a folder filled by hand could hold. This file guards the folder `npm run build:web` writes and
# nothing else: in a checkout it sits in public/, where no host reads it, so a host pointed at the
# checkout serves every file in it (only deploy/nginx.conf, which lives in the server's own
# configuration, refuses them there). Cloudflare Pages ignores 404 rules (publish the folder the
# build writes there, which leaves metadata.json out) and serves .htaccess and .nojekyll as files;
# GitHub Pages serves all four configuration files. Nothing in any of them is secret.
/_headers        /404.html  404!
/_redirects      /404.html  404!
/.htaccess       /404.html  404!
/.nojekyll       /404.html  404!
/metadata.json   /404.html  404!
/README.md       /404.html  404!
/.git/*          /404.html  404!
/deploy/*        /404.html  404!
